1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Dad was born January 19, 1913 to Trygve J. Magelssen and Audrey Lee Richmond. He had black hair, denim blue eyes and was of a medium build. He was strong and athletic. I remember his athleticism frustrating my brot
2 CONC hers when they issued physical challenges. Their favorite challenges were arm wrestling, pull-ups, and chin-ups, on the branch of the tree right outside the back door and once in a while a foot race along the side of the house. Th
2 CONC ey would say "come on old man let's see what you've got". This always made me smile...because much to their dismay they usually LOST! haha (of course, I am sure Dad quit taking the challenges when he could no longer win!)
2 CONT
2 CONT I would like to include here a few words written by Dad. I received these in a file that my older sister Shirley gave me.
2 CONT
2 CONT "I am Earl Alton Monroe Magelsen born in Tacoma, Pierce County State of Washington on the 19th of January 1913. My father is Trgve J. Magelssen who was born in Ellinggard Ostfold,Norway the 28th of March 1881. His Dad and Mother (
2 CONC Jens Christian Meinich Magelssen and Thora Marie Magelssen) came to America the month of November 1887. My father's family settled in Highland Prairie Minnesota on a farm."
2 CONT
2 CONT I would note here that Dad's grandparents came before their children. The girls came in 1888 all except Ingeborg. I can place her here by 1894. The baby Wilhelm, I believe came with the girls. Maria-13 yrs Aslaug- 4 yrs, Borghild 
2 CONC 3 yrs and Wilhelm 2 yrs. Next in 1890 Thorbjorn and Finn came they were 13 and 12 yrs of age. Trygve and Frithjof were last. They didn't immigrate until August 20th 1896...nine years after their parents! They were 15 and 13 years 
2 CONC of age. Maria lived with her Uncle Nils Magelssen, Thorbjorn lived with his Aunt Edvardine Aschenberg and Finn stayed with his Uncle Christian Magelssen. Trygve and Frithjof lived with their Aunt Marthe Margrethe Frimann Magelssen
2 CONC  Ottesen,
2 CONT
2 CONT Dad's written words..."Dad came to America in 1895 (note: records say August of 1896) After a few years with his parents on the farm he came to the west coast where he met and married my mother Audrey Lee Richmond, on the 25th of 
2 CONC January 1912. My mother was born in Raleigh, Crow County West Virginia USA. Her mother and father were Laura Belle Underwood and Oliver R Richmond and her grandmother and grandfather were James Richmond and Lucinda Walker all were
2 CONC  born in West Virginia."
2 CONT
2 CONT "As I sit here and try to write my personal history I am getting a profound spiritual influence in my being of the great blessing of life in mortality. The priviledge of raising my children was the greatest joy of my life."
2 CONT
2 CONT "When mother gave birth to me I either had pneumonia or contracted it. Dad's sister Marie was there and on orders from Doctor Monroe (my middle name came from him) they dipped me in hot and cold water one after the other to keep m
2 CONC e alive as the story was told me to stimulate my circulation. Dad said that Aunt Marie finally cooked up a big fry pan of onions which was put around my feet. Dad told me that you could see the blue, beginning at my head and reced
2 CONC ing down , leave my body."
2 CONT
2 CONT "The home I was born in was at 3251 South Wilkersen. It is now covered by the highway that runs from Tacoma to South Tacoma. My home there was a two-story house setting quite high off the ground. It had an open foundation so the w
2 CONC ind could get under it real easy and rock a person to sleep."
2 CONT
2 CONT "As a child I can remember one time when we had a wind and rain storm and my sisters got real scared. Mama said she was sure everything would be all right, but I could see the fear in her face. Boy that house could sure rock and g
2 CONC roan. Being high off the ground it had some advantages though. I used to coast my wagon and homemade scooter under the back porch which was also open. That was my garage! (big Stuff) I took a 1/2 lb. coffee can and nailed it to th
2 CONC e radiator of my homemade car put a candle in it and had a light at night. A kid's world is a special place."
2 CONT
2 CONT "I was sick a lot in first grade: My tonsils were bad and I had to have them removed."
2 CONT
2 CONT "My Dad and Mother separated when I was approximately seven years of age. I have two sisters Mildred and Velma both younger than I who stayed with mother until her death. They later went to live with Dad in Everett WA. Mother marr
2 CONC ied again. She married a man by the name of Gaither W. Freeman who had three sons by a previous marriage. The oldest son was Howard then, Lester and Elmer. Howard and Lester came to live with my two sisters when their Dad and my m
2 CONC other united."
2 CONT
2 CONT *(I would note here that Gaither and Audrey didn't marry unil Dec. 2nd 1927 about 6 months before her death on May 29 1928. How long they lived together I don't know. Pictures place them together by 1922)
2 CONT
2 CONT Dad continued..."When Dad and Mother separated, I went to live with Dad and ended up at his brother's home in Stanwood Washington. I think I was there a couple of years. During this period of time I also stayed with my Dad possibl
2 CONC y two years. I finally was sent to live with Mother's brother Paul Richmond and his first wife Hilda on a wheat ranch in the Horse Heaven Hills south west of Kennewick Washingtron As a whole I was pretty happy there."
2 CONT
2 CONT Dad went on to say that he attended first grade at Whitman in Tacoma. He finished 2nd grade in Stanwood at his Uncle Thorbjorn and Aunt Ida's home.
2 CONT
2 CONT He wrote of this experience..."We all did chores around there. That is one thing about a farm you all work. I learned to milk and was milking four and five cow's night and morning. I can remember that they were not giving much mil
2 CONC k however; I can remember Uncle being upset because the udder or bag on one of the cows got hard because I didn't milk her dry. That was quite a place to stay. I cannot ever remember doing anything right. I heard a lot of talk abo
2 CONC ut my mother which I didn't appreciate. I guess I was the fall guy because of her and Dad not getting along. To me mother was one of the best. She told me that she married Dad to get away from home. I am sure that she had a better
2 CONC  feeling for Dad than that. Dad loved her with all his heart. He just made a big mistake in his life which contributed to him and Mother not being able to live together."
2 CONT
2 CONT *Trygve was a saleman and from what mom told me he had a sexual encounter that left him with a sexually transmitted disease, which at that time there was no cure for.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dad writes of his experience at his Uncle Paul's..."When I first went there to live, we would not get into town for two weeks to a month at a time. We didn't have a car and drove a team and Buckboard (wagon) in one day and bought 
2 CONC supplies. We would stay overnight and drive the team back to the ranch the next day."
2 CONT
2 CONT *Living with Paul was the end of schooling for Dad.
2 CONT
2 CONT "In those days the roads were so bad, (they were all dirt with no surface gravel or anything) that the wheels on the wagon would be moving in twelve to eighteen inches of light soil or dirt. Many of the trips into town were made b
2 CONC y my Aunt Hilda and me. In those days there were several hundred wild horses in the horse heavens that were owned by different individuals. My Uncle Paul bought what he needed of these wild horses and broke them to work."
2 CONT
2 CONT He goes on..."I just must tell you about Jim. He was a horse we worked in the wheel on the wagon or water tank. He had to haul water. Jim had personality and was always ready to work or play. It didn't seem that Jim ever got tired
2 CONC . He was an easy keeper, always in good condition and sassy. I can remember many of his antics. One time my Uncle Paul hired a man for spring work, old Jim spotted him when he went through the fence into the horse corral and waite
2 CONC d with his head up until he was in about the center of the corral. He then opened his mouth and bared his teeth. He put his head down and proceeded to run the hired man out of the corral. (Which he did, mission accomplished) Jim w
2 CONC as just bluffing but he made a big thing of it and that hired man was taken to town the first of the week. Jim knew he had his number and wouldn't let the hired man handle him."
2 CONT
2 CONT This was the end of dad's writing. I wished he would have written more.
2 CONT
2 CONT I would like to insert here that whatever did or didn't happen to Dad as he grew up impacted him. Because of his childhood we had some solid and steadfast rules in our home. We were not allowed to fight or bicker amongst ourselves
2 CONC , or bully one another. Whatever the grown-ups were having or doing the children were included. If we as children went to the store for a candy bar or pizza etc...and we came home beore it was finished...we had better be prepared 
2 CONC to share. There was not getting up and having a bowl of ice cream or popcorn or a sandwich, without offering and fixing some for everyone else. If we were going out to dinner or the movies and we had a friend over they were invite
2 CONC d and Dad picked up the tab.
2 CONT
2 CONT I remember when Dad was in his 70's living in Pasco Wa. I happened to walk in on him and mom, they were watching TV. A Mormon or Catholic ad was on the tv, I don't remember which church sponsored it. In the commercial there was an
2 CONC  old Model T pickup headed to town loaded with a bunch of boys in overalls sitting on the back tailgate. They were taunting and making fun of a boy who had been left behind. This boy was running up the dirt road trying to catch th
2 CONC em. The little boy was distraught and he collapses to his knees crying. I looked over at Dad and tears were streaming down his cheeks. It broke my heart to realize that, he not only identified with the little boy that was left beh
2 CONC ind but the hurt was so deep that 60 years later it still brought such anguish to him that it caused him to weep. (that was the only time I remember seeing him cry)
2 CONT
2 CONT There were times in our home when we had the opportunity to have children live with us who weren't related to us. I know that Bobby Jean Yates lived for a year with Mom and Dad when Shirley and Jim were kids. Her father had worked
2 CONC  for Dad. Then her children LeAndra and Darryl lived with us when I was in 7th grade. They also stayed for 12 months. (Bobby Jean and the children stayed in touch with mom and dad for the rest of their lives) Also Tammy had a frie
2 CONC nd Cheryl Mohart who lived with mom and Dad her senior year in HIgh School.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dad also watched out for and included children from our church. I remember a time when we went on vaction to Curlew Lake. He was a home teacher to a family who had recently divorced and were going through a tough time. He brought 
2 CONC Richard, (he was between Jens and I in age) along with us. He wanted him to have a happy family experiece or perhaps just something to take his mind off what was happening at home.
2 CONT
2 CONT One night as a child, we were playing hide and go seek with all the neighborhood kids outside. Our home was the central gathering spot of the neighborhood. I am sure part of that was because of the 4 acres of land that our home wa
2 CONC s on. One little girl fell and gashed her head open. Her mother was divorced and didn't have much financially. Their home was about the size of single car garage. Looking back, I now realize how little they had. Dad just scopped J
2 CONC ane up and took her to the emergency room had her stitched up and paid the expense. We absolutely could not do that now but times were simpler then.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dad had his faults but he had his strengths too. He was very giving with what he had. He always looked out for those around him who were in need. He had a very tender spot especially for those children who came from broken homes. 
2 CONC I feel blessed to call him father and I am grateful for the example he set.
2 CONT
2 CONT I remember kneeling for every prayer even when we blessed the food at the dinner table. As a child everyone hated to be the person that sat at the back in the middle of the table. It was crowded, extremely hard to kneel there and 
2 CONC Jens was usually the poor soul designated to that spot. Sorry Jens:)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Family prayers and father's blessings were part of my life with Dad. Bless his heart he even tried to have a night devoted just to the family, where he would teach about Christ, play games and have treats. That didn't always work 
2 CONC out but he did try.
2 CONT
2 CONT One memory I have was when I was very sick and running a high fever. It was on a Sunday night when I was probably about 4 maybe 5 years old. Everyone went to church except Dad and I. He gave me a blessing then picked me up and hel
2 CONC d me tight in his arms. He walked the floor that night with me, my head cradled on his shoulder. I remember the chandelier seeming to sway as we walked and his voice soothing me as he prayed to our Father in Heaven for my safe rec
2 CONC overy. He had the gift of healing and a vast amount of faith. By the time the family returned from church my fever had broken. (When I was older, I asked mom about this memory, she said that I had been extremely ill with Scarlet F
2 CONC ever and was completely recovered when she returned from church.)
2 CONT
2 CONT No matter how upset as a child or teenager I became with my Dad, and I remember that as being pretty easy, he was my refuge. I knew that where he was, I would be protected from the ugliness of the world. Our home and my father wer
2 CONC e my safe haven.
2 CONT
2 CONT I am assuming that after life with Paul is when Dad goes to the Yakima area to work in the hop fields. While working in that area he meets Edith Crystal Jordan. (Aka Mom) They happened to attend the same dance in the Horse Heaven 
2 CONC Hills. He stated that as he glanced across the room at Edith, he heard a voice that said, "She is to be the mother of your children". He married Mom on February 20, 1937 in Kennewick Washington. They had planned on and were on the
2 CONC ir way to Bickleton to get married. That was where her family lived. However, there was a snow storm that day which closed the road into Bickleton. They were forced to stop. They found a phone and called grandma and grandpa, who t
2 CONC old them to go ahead and get married! Ha-ha I think Dad was thinking he had dodged the bullet!!! But Grace called the Justice of the Peace in Franklin County. Mom and Dad were married with Rollie and Grace Waggoner as their witnes
2 CONC ses. (mom's sister and brother-in-law)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mom proved to be the greatest thing that ever happened to Dad. She taught him how to love and trust and what family is and was all about. Dad really didn’t understand what a family was before mom.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Dad's first job after marriage was in Grand Coulee Washington, their home a TENT! He worked as part of the construction crew on The Grand Coulee Dam. However, after some near-death experiences involving large boulders dropping too
2 CONC  close for comfort, they decided to move to the Seattle Washington area where he worked at the Navy base as an auto mechanic and detailer.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Entrepreneurship ran in Dads veins. He owned the Pollyanna Cafe in Kennewick Washington which he sold to his brother-in-law Alfred Donahue. He owned a Jukebox and pinball machine business, and a company (Shirley said) that brought
2 CONC  background music into shopping malls and stores, (I wonder if she got this confused with his tape vending routes?) a trucking company (his partner in that adventure was Eldon Matthews) and Magelsen Service Tire, all located and b
2 CONC ased in Kennewick Washington. He owned a Music Store in Pasco Washington, and a couple of different cassette tape vendor routes which were also based out of Kennewick. He also owned and rented several multiple family properties.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Dad and Mom joined The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day Saints on October 5, 1951. They devoted the rest of their lives to serving their Savior.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He and Mom in their later years served four consecutive Seattle Temple missions for the church. Dad served a full-time stake mission shortly after they joined the church 1952-1954. He served a second stake mission from 1960-1962. 
2 CONC Mom served as a genealogical librarian for two years. They both served a mission as employment specialists after their Temple missions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dad and Mom bought a vacation home on Twin Lakes in Ferry County Washington. Dad loved to fish and he wanted a place where the family could have fun together. The family did spend many joyful years creating fun memories. In fact, 
2 CONC this is where dad spent the last week of his life surrounded by his family.
2 CONT
2 CONT The last day at the lake Kyndra (my youngest daughter) just wouldn’t leave Dad alone. She had just turned one, she was a willful little thing and DEMANDED that Dad (her grandfather) hold her on his lap. She would stand on his knee
2 CONC s and take his face in her hands and do that baby open mouth kiss all over his face. He would set her down and she would demand to be up again. She would turn around and start kissing him again! She absolutely would NOT leave him 
2 CONC alone. I kept apologizing to Dad but he didn’t seem to mind. I wonder now if she knew or could sense something the rest of us didn’t.
2 CONT
2 CONT I was the primary care giver to Dad. Bill offered while we were all up at the lake to bring Dad home and care for him a few days to give me and my family a chance to spend some much-needed time together. It was a very sweet gestur
2 CONC e and much appreciated. Nevertheless, I will admit my heart broke when Bill called and told me that dad had passed away. I rushed home to say my goodbyes before they took the body from my home.
2 CONT
2 CONT My last living memory of Dad was at the lake when he was getting into Bill’s vehicle. I gave him a big hug told him I loved him and would be home soon. Then, when I stepped back and looked at him I said “Dad, oh my GOSH where is y
2 CONC our tooth?!” His front gold backed tooth had fallen out. He smiled patted the front pocket of his shirt and said Christy don’t worry I have it right here. I have always wondered what happened to it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dad died of kidney failure on August 17, 1994, in my home on West 23rd Avenue in Kennewick Washington. Mother died December 31, 1993 in their home in Pasco Washington, from breast cancer. They were married 56 years.
