1 NAME Hans Peter Faye /L'Orange/
2 GIVN Hans Peter Faye
2 _MARNM L'Orange
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 15 FEB 1892
2 ADDR
3 CITY Quincy
3 STAE Florida
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE JAN 1984
2 ADDR
3 CITY Wahiawā
3 STAE Hawaii
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Hans L'Orange as told by a friend.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT When I started working at Oahu Sugar Company in 1931, Hans was the Field Superintendent. In 1936 I was working as a machinist in the Machine Shop. Oscar Myhre, the Night Engineer, died, and our manager, E.W. Green, was in Washingt
2 CONC on D.C. Hans was Acting Manager.  
2 CONT
2 CONT L'Orange wanted to promote me to Night Engineer, but had to get the "okay" from Mr. Green. Once this was done, I started in my new position. Not long after, the Hawaii Sugar Planters Association (HSPA) took Green away from OSCO. T
2 CONC hey wanted him to be a lobbyist for them in Washington D.C.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans became Manager. I always said that he and I started in more responsible jobs about the same time. We became very good friends.  
2 CONT
2 CONT One day we were having quite a problem in the factory. In fact, it was shut down. A group of us, including Sugar Boiler Bromley and Factory Supt. Johnston were energetically trying to find the source of the problem: loss of vacuum
2 CONC  in the boiling operation.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans came over and started complaining to me. I didn't appreciate his complaining, since we were doing out best to find and fix the problem, and I told him so. As he left the factory, I wondered if he would fire me for the way I t
2 CONC alked to him.  
2 CONT
2 CONT After the problem was solved and everything was running smoothly, I went over to his office and apologized for speaking to him as I did. Hans smiled and said, "Oh, that's okay, Jack. It's good to blow off steam once in a while. Fo
2 CONC rget it."  
2 CONT
2 CONT Another time our kitchen stove broke down, and I decided to walk to the Plantation Store on Waipahu St. to look at new stoves. I met Hans along the way. He wanted to know what I was doing. I explained that I was thinking about buy
2 CONC ing a new stove because ours was broken, and he said, "Go ahead and buy it, Jack. You’re getting a raise this month."  
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans loved throwing parties in his home. He was a great supporter of the U.S. Navy, and during WWII he often entertained Navy officers. He also occasionally invited OSCO supervisors to join them. I met Admiral Chester Nimitz and o
2 CONC ther ranking officers at some of these parties.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after the war, I was promoted to Milling Engineer. The year OSCO had its biggest crop 81,000 tons L’Orange decided to slaughter two plantation Black Angus and a pig and throw a barbeque for all the plantation employees and
2 CONC  their families. I helped design and create three motorized spits for the beef and pork, and also supervised all the cooking Great party!  
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans loved sports, and opened an area up on the plantation so employees could have a place to participate in various athletic events.  Mrs. L'Orange (Mellie) was also a good friend. She was a lovely lady and we all loved her.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT http://search.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/sse.dll?db=1940usfedcen&indiv=try&h=78917623
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans had 6 siblings: Cecilie Beta Alexandra Knudsen (born L'Orange), Arvid Knudsen L'Orange and 4 other siblings.  [https://www.myheritage.com/names/hans_lorange]
2 CONT
2 CONT Story by Tim Vogel.   It was told by my uncle Peter L'Orange, Hans' son, that Admiral Chester Nimitz and his staff were frequent guests at their mountain house that over looked Pearl from several miles away.  My great grand uncle 
2 CONC was head of Civil Defense at that time.  He was also manager of the Oahu Sugar Company.  My uncle Pete said that as a boy of 10 years he was terrified during the attack. He recalled how the planes roared overhead very close to the
2 CONC  rooftops.  The Japanese used this house as a "line of sight" to the harbor, much like a "gun sight."  Peter hid under the coach.  The house received shrapnel from exploding shells from the harbor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Three months earlier Peter and a field hand from the sugar plantation were walking on the beach and found an abandoned Japanese mini-sub. They tracked the sailor and captured them in the sugar cane field. They were handed over to 
2 CONC the authorities.  Nothing else was mentioned about it.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===========
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans “Pete” Peter Faye L’Orange Jr., 83, of Waimea, died March 27, 2017, at North Hawaii Community Hospital. Born Oct. 17, 1933, in Waipahu, Oahu, he was a former rancher for Parker Ranch and McCandless Ranch, former executive dir
2 CONC ector of Hawaii Leeward Planning Conference, a football coach and a community housing advocate. He was a member of St. James Episcopal Church and was dedicated to a number of community organizations including the Hawaii Island Com
2 CONC munity Development Corporation, Kona Soil and Water Conservation District, Lion’s Club, Hawaii Island Burial Council, Hawaii County Liquor Commission and Hawaii Cattlemen’s Association.
